<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harun al-Rashid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/786">786</a>–<a href="/years/809">809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harun al-Rashid was Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> who reigned from 786 to 809. His reign is remembered as a high point of Abbasid prosperity and culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and is immortalised in The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Thousand and One Nights</a>. He exchanged embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="Harun al-Rashid" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 8/9th-century master(s) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Harun_al-Rashid,_AH_170-193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harun was born in Rey, the son of the third Abbasid caliph, al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khayzuran">al-Khayzuran</a>, a woman of strong character who greatly influenced affairs of state under her husband and sons. His birth date is debated, with sources giving dates from 763 to 766. He devoted most of his time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and trained in swordplay and archery. In 780 and again in 782 he nominally led campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> under Empress Irene; the second reached the Asian suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His raids raised his standing and earned him the title al-Rashid, the Rightly-Guided One. He was made crown prince and given the western territories from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Harun became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in September 786, in his early twenties, when his brother al-Hadi died. On his accession he led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>'s Great Mosque. His mother kept a strong hand in government until her death in 789. His vizier Yahya ibn Khalid and Yahya's sons, especially Ja'far, of the Iranian family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a>, generally controlled the administration, and the position of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at court peaked in his reign. Their fall is more likely due to behaviour Harun found disrespectful, such as entering his court unannounced and deciding matters of state without consulting him; al-Fadl ibn al-Rabi succeeded Yahya as chief minister. In 796 he moved his court and government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>.</p><p>Envoys travelled between his court and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, discussing Christian access to holy sites and exchanging gifts. In 802 Harun sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> chessmen, a large tent, an elephant named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas">Abul-Abbas</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-clock">water clock</a> whose mechanical knights emerged one for each hour. Both rulers had common interests: Charlemagne's conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> and the Byzantine opposition to his claim to the imperial title. In 805 Harun defeated Emperor Nikephoros I at the Battle of Krasos, and in 807 his forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A major revolt led by Rafi ibn al-Layth in Samarqand drew Harun east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in 808. He became ill and died in March 809 at Sanabad in Tus, and was buried in the summer palace of the governor, Humayd ibn Qahtaba. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> succeeded him. The place later became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>. Anecdotes gathered round him for centuries, and Saadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> put several into his Gulistan, while parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a> are set in his court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">Al-Masudi</a> wrote that the splendour and riches of his reign were such that the period was called the Honeymoon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harun al-Rashid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Barmakid family belonged to this mixed elite: they were Persian administrators who served the early caliphs and rose to the height of their power as viziers under <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> until their sudden fall in 803.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 800 the Abbasid caliph <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> appointed Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab, the son of a Khurasani Arab commander of the Banu Tamim tribe, hereditary emir of Ifriqiya, in answer to the anarchy that had followed the fall of the Muhallabids in that province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne exchanged embassies with the Abbasid caliph <b>Harun al-Rashid</b>, an exchange that brought the elephant Abul-Abbas to Aachen in 802.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> the region was governed by Yazid ibn Mazyad al-Shaybani, an Arab commander whose family, the Yazidids or Mazyadids, put down roots there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Common protagonists are the Abbasid caliph <b>Harun al-Rashid</b>, his vizier Jafar al-Barmaki and the poet Abu Nuwas, and a character often tells a story within which another is told.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Within two generations, under <b>Harun al-Rashid</b> (r. 786–809) and al-Ma'mun (r. 813–833), the caliphate presided over the richest and most sophisticated society in western Eurasia.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ray, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Tus, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.6,51.43&amp;year=797" aria-label="Ray on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5849 878 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harun al-Rashid"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6299 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6299 1148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.6,51.43&amp;year=797">What was here in 797 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-khayzuran"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Al-Khayzuran</span><span class="tp">Mother &#183; d. 789</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/zubaidah-bint-jafar"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Zubaidah bint Ja'far</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; d. 831</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-amin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-amin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">al-Amin</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 787–813</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mamun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">al-Ma'mun</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; r. 813–833</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mutasim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">al-Mu'tasim</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; r. 833–842</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have read thy letter, O thou son of an unbelieving mother. Thou shalt not hear, thou shalt behold, my reply.&#8221;</p><footer>Answer to the emperor Nikephoros I, 803 (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 52) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it; the reply was a campaign into Asia Minor &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/735/pg735-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
